#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:5
msgid "Customizing the Extensible SDK standalone installer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:7
msgid ""
"This appendix describes customizations you can apply to the extensible "
"SDK when using in the standalone installer version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:12
msgid ""
"It is also possible to use the Extensible SDK functionality directly in a"
" Yocto build, avoiding separate installer artefacts. Please refer to "
"\":ref:`sdk-manual/extensible:Installing the Extensible SDK`\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:17
msgid "Configuring the Extensible SDK"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:19
msgid ""
"The extensible SDK primarily consists of a pre-configured copy of the "
"OpenEmbedded build system from which it was produced. Thus, the SDK's "
"configuration is derived using that build system and the filters shown in"
" the following list. When these filters are present, the OpenEmbedded "
"build system applies them against :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` "
"and :ref:`structure-build-conf-auto.conf`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:26
msgid ""
"Variables whose values start with \"/\" are excluded since the assumption"
" is that those values are paths that are likely to be specific to the "
":term:`Build Host`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:30
msgid ""
"Variables listed in :term:`ESDK_LOCALCONF_REMOVE` are excluded. These "
"variables are not allowed through from the OpenEmbedded build system "
"configuration into the extensible SDK configuration. Typically, these "
"variables are specific to the machine on which the build system is "
"running and could be problematic as part of the extensible SDK "
"configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:38
msgid ""
"For a list of the variables excluded by default, see the "
":term:`ESDK_LOCALCONF_REMOVE` in the glossary of the Yocto Project "
"Reference Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:42
msgid ""
"Variables listed in :term:`ESDK_LOCALCONF_ALLOW` are included. Including "
"a variable in the value of :term:`ESDK_LOCALCONF_ALLOW` overrides either "
"of the previous two filters. The default value is blank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:48
msgid ""
"Classes inherited globally with :term:`INHERIT` that are listed in "
":term:`ESDK_CLASS_INHERIT_DISABLE` are disabled. Using "
":term:`ESDK_CLASS_INHERIT_DISABLE` to disable these classes is the "
"typical method to disable classes that are problematic or unnecessary in "
"the SDK context. The default value disables the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"buildhistory` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:55
msgid ""
"Additionally, the contents of ``conf/sdk-extra.conf``, when present, are "
"appended to the end of :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` within the "
"produced SDK, without any filtering. The ``sdk-extra.conf`` file is "
"particularly useful if you want to set a variable value just for the SDK "
"and not the OpenEmbedded build system used to create the SDK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:62
msgid "Adjusting the Extensible SDK to Suit Your Build Host's Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:64
msgid ""
"In most cases, the extensible SDK defaults should work with your "
":term:`Build Host`'s setup. However, there are cases when you might "
"consider making adjustments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:68
msgid ""
"If your SDK configuration inherits additional classes using the "
":term:`INHERIT` variable and you do not need or want those classes "
"enabled in the SDK, you can disable them by adding them to the "
":term:`ESDK_CLASS_INHERIT_DISABLE` variable as described in the previous "
"section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:76
msgid ""
"The default value of :term:`ESDK_CLASS_INHERIT_DISABLE` is set using the "
"\"?=\" operator. Consequently, you will need to either define the entire "
"list by using the \"=\" operator, or you will need to append a value "
"using either \":append\" or the \"+=\" operator. You can learn more about"
" these operators in the \":ref:`bitbake-user-manual/bitbake-user-manual-"
"metadata:basic syntax`\" section of the BitBake User Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:84
msgid ""
"If you have classes or recipes that add additional tasks to the standard "
"build flow (i.e. the tasks execute as the recipe builds as opposed to "
"being called explicitly), then you need to do one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:89
msgid ""
"After ensuring the tasks are :ref:`shared state <overview-"
"manual/concepts:shared state cache>` tasks (i.e. the output of the task "
"is saved to and can be restored from the shared state cache) or ensuring "
"the tasks are able to be produced quickly from a task that is a shared "
"state task, add the task name to the value of :term:`SDK_RECRDEP_TASKS`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:97
msgid ""
"Disable the tasks if they are added by a class and you do not need the "
"functionality the class provides in the extensible SDK. To disable the "
"tasks, add the class to the :term:`ESDK_CLASS_INHERIT_DISABLE` variable "
"as described in the previous section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:102
msgid ""
"Generally, you want to have a shared state mirror set up so users of the "
"SDK can add additional items to the SDK after installation without "
"needing to build the items from source. See the \":ref:`sdk-manual"
"/appendix-customizing:providing additional installable extensible sdk "
"content`\" section for information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:108
msgid ""
"If you want users of the SDK to be able to easily update the SDK, you "
"need to set the :term:`SDK_UPDATE_URL` variable. For more information, "
"see the \":ref:`sdk-manual/appendix-customizing:providing updates to the "
"extensible sdk after installation`\" section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:115
msgid ""
"If you have adjusted the list of files and directories that appear in "
":term:`COREBASE` (other than layers that are enabled through :ref"
":`structure-build-conf-bblayers.conf`), then you must list these files in"
" :term:`COREBASE_FILES` so that the files are copied into the SDK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:122
msgid ""
"If your OpenEmbedded build system setup uses a different environment "
"setup script other than :ref:`structure-core-script`, then you must set "
":term:`OE_INIT_ENV_SCRIPT` to point to the environment setup script you "
"use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:131
msgid ""
"You must also reflect this change in the value used for the "
":term:`COREBASE_FILES` variable as previously described."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:135
msgid "Changing the Extensible SDK Installer Title"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:137
msgid ""
"You can change the displayed title for the SDK installer by setting the "
":term:`SDK_TITLE` variable and then rebuilding the SDK installer. For "
"information on how to build an SDK installer, see the \":ref:`sdk-manual"
"/appendix-obtain:building an sdk installer`\" section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:143
msgid ""
"By default, this title is derived from :term:`DISTRO_NAME` when it is "
"set. If the :term:`DISTRO_NAME` variable is not set, the title is derived"
" from the :term:`DISTRO` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:148
msgid ""
"The :ref:`populate_sdk_base <ref-classes-populate-sdk-*>` class defines "
"the default value of the :term:`SDK_TITLE` variable as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/appendix-customizing.rst:157
msgid ""
"While there are several ways of changing this variable, an efficient "
"method is to set the variable in your distribution's configuration file. "
"Doing so creates an SDK installer title that applies across your "
"distribution. As an example, assume you have your own layer for your "
