"Extensible SDK can be installed in two different ways, and both have "
"their own pros and cons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/extensible.rst:50
msgid ""
"*Setting up the Extensible SDK environment directly in a Yocto build*. "
"This avoids having to produce, test, distribute and maintain separate SDK"
" installer archives, which can get very large. There is only one "
"environment for the regular Yocto build and the SDK and less code paths "
"where things can go not according to plan. It's easier to update the SDK:"
" it simply means updating the Yocto layers with git fetch or layer "
"management tooling. The SDK extensibility is better than in the second "
"option: just run ``bitbake`` again to add more things to the sysroot, or "
"add layers if even more things are required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../sdk-manual/extensible.rst:60
msgid ""
"*Setting up the Extensible SDK from a standalone installer*. This has the"
" benefit of having a single, self-contained archive that includes all the"
" needed binary artifacts. So nothing needs to be rebuilt, and there is no"
" need to provide a well-functioning binary artefact cache over the "
"network for developers with underpowered laptops."
msgstr ""

